Changes to the NML package introduced from October 1st to December 1st, 2004
============================================================================
Marek Korkusinski, marekk@purdue.edu


1. Implemented new memory allocation/deallocation routines
nml_malloc(), nml_calloc(), nml_free()
These functions are declared in the file /src/global/global.hP
Their implementation can be found in the file /src/global/nml_memory.c
Their basic functionality is prepending the allocated memory block with a field
containing information about the size of this block (in bytes).
This is realized by defining a struct, in which the first field is the size-holding variable,
and the second field is the beginning of the block.
The allocation routines return the pointer to the actual memory block (and not to the 
prepended field), but the offset (in bytes) necessary to move the pointer back to the size field
is stored.
The idea of these memory constructs is due to Michael Mclennan.

The nml_free() frees the allocated memory correctly (i.e., with the size field).
Additionally, global memory control variables are introduced:
active_memory = showing the current total memory allocation (i.e., the amount of memory
allocated for all data structures)
max_active_memory = high-water mark, showing the maximal total memory allocation (i.e., maximal
active_memory over the program's runtime)
freed_memory = showing the total amount of memory ever freed
total_memory = showing the total amount of memory ever allocated
The function nml_memory_report(char *) prints all these memory indicators to standard output, followed
by the string given to this function as the argument.
These memory management tools are switched on ONLY if the package is compiled
with option -DNML_MEM_EXT
Otherwise, NML uses the system's functions malloc(), calloc() and free(), maintains memory indicators
at zero, and does nothing when nml_memory_report() is called.

2. Implemented new constructs to handle the vector size field.
In NML vectors, templated in files /src/vector/vector.[hP|cP] and /src/vector/1DBase.[hP|cP],
allocation of a vector of n fields would usually be realized by allocating (n+1) fields and
writing the number of elements (n) into the element 0.
However, for arrays of chars on 32-bit platforms, and also other types on 64-bit platforms,
this management is insufficient: you cannot put an int (or a long) into a char.
The problem was solved by allocating (n) fields and prepending it with a field of size_t,
in the manner identical to that used in the memory allocation/deallocation routines 
described in point 1.
Since the design is introduced into the patterns, it is propagated to vectors of all types;
vectors of each type have a unique structure to hold the information.
The constructors and destructors of vectors, as well as vector size extractors have been modified 
to take the new structures fully into account.

The constructs are turned on in /src/vector/vector.hP and /src/vector/1DBase.hP
by defining the compilation conditional option EXT_MEM_HANDLING
As of now, this option is turned on in these files, so it is not necessary to include it as a compiler 
option.
When it is #undef'ed, the package will fully return to the previous allocation schemes.

NOTE 1
======
The new structures have been implemented for vectors only.
For matrix and tensor allocations they are not necessary, as data structures used there are
vectors of pointers (of size_t), so overflow is not expected on any platform.

NOTE 2
=======
This management makes it impossible to use the constructs "vdim" on vectors.
If one wants to extract the vector length, one should use the proper NML function
nml_<type>v_extent()
However, vdim(), rdim() and cdim() will work on matrices and tensors.

3. Implemented new types.
In keeping with NML's general formats, each new type has assigned a letter unique to it.
The new types are:

precision/type                 letter
==========================================
char                            b
short                           s
single                          f
single complex                  k, fc

The appropriate system of Makefiles, header files and source code has been created
for each of these types on all levels (from scalar to tensor).
This system closely follows the system laid out for the other types, existing
originally within NML (i.e., double, double complex, integer).
Moreover, the NML file generator genclass (in /bin/) was tweaked to accomodate the new letters.
